From ef4658f3b2f0b6e42d999ae927b689837c417fd9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: haowen1993 <39072333+haowen1993@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 09:15:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] feat(engine): implement the CR 607.2p pregame colour
 choice for a commander
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

"Impossible Girl — If Clara Oswald is your commander, choose a color before the
game begins. Clara Oswald is the chosen color." (Clara Oswald) lowers to a LINKED
pair per CR 607.2p: a `BeginGame` ability that asks, and a characteristic-defining
static that reads the answer. Measured over the whole corpus (Scryfall
`oracle:"choose a color before the game begins"`, `unique=cards`) the class is
exactly three cards — Clara Oswald, Faceless One, The Prismatic Piper — and all
three word it identically apart from the name, so the recognizer covers the class
rather than the card.

Four defects had to be fixed, three of them general:

  * The card-data setter is not a zone. CR 604.3 makes a CDA function in every
    zone, but `effect_candidate_ids` resolved a named-object filter against the
    FILTER's scan zones, which default to the battlefield. An admitted `SelfRef`
    CDA on a card in the command zone therefore had an EMPTY affected set and was
    dropped outright — not evaluated false, never executed. Fixed for the
    `named == source_id` case only: a `SpecificObject` naming some OTHER object
    keeps the scan-zone gate, because that class is delivered by the off-zone
    authority (pinned by
    `identity_shortcut_declines_for_a_predicate_and_spans_non_battlefield_zones`,
    whose graveyard-bound cross-object grant must not start landing here).

  * The pregame drain scanned only the opening hand. CR 903.6 puts a commander in
    the command zone before the first turn, so a commander's own pregame ability
    could never be found. `queue_begin_game_abilities` now scans hand then command
    zone, per player.

  * `StaticCondition` had no way to say "the source is a commander". Added
    `SourceIsCommander` (CR 903.3: the designation is an attribute of the card and
    holds in every zone) and wired all 20 exhaustive-match sites.

  * The chosen colour needs a store that survives a zone change. CR 607.2p says
    the linked reference "continues to refer to that choice as the object changes
    zones", but `chosen_attributes` is cleared by `reset_for_battlefield_entry`
    (CR 400.7) on every battlefield entry — a commander chosen in the command zone
    would lose its colour the moment it resolved. Added
    `GameObject::commander_color_choice`, written by the `Choose` resolver during
    the pregame drain and read FIRST by `GameObject::chosen_color`, so the whole
    chosen-colour family (`AddChosenColor`, `IsChosenColor`,
    `HexproofFrom`/`Protection(ChosenColor)`) serves this card unchanged.

KNOWN GAP, deliberately deferred and recorded rather than silently accepted —
CR 903.4b: the chosen colour "applies during deck construction ... That choice
affects the commander's color identity." Deck validation does not know the choice,
so with Clara as a commander a custom deck is not colour-identity-checked against
it (CR 903.5c/903.5d). Reach is one scenario this fork's four precons never take:
Paradox Power's commanders are The Thirteenth Doctor and Yasmin Khan, and Clara
sits in the 99 where CR 903.3d makes her ability's commander gate FALSE — the
whole paragraph is inert and the engine already handles her correctly. Closing the
gap needs deck-side colour selection (a `DeckCompatibilityRequest` /
`PlayerDeckList` field plus a deck-builder picker) and is its own unit of work.

Unrelated pre-existing gap found while tracing the above, not fixed here:
`deck_validation::card_color_identity`'s fallback reads only the mana cost and
`color_override`, never mana symbols in rules text (CR 903.4), and never a CDA.
That is masked in this fork because the data pipeline pre-fills `color_identity`
from Scryfall for 281 of 362 WHO cards and the fallback only runs for the other 81.
